 {"text": "Part C Page 19", "x0": 0.5593406593406594, "y0": 0.7143968871595331, "x1": 0.7615384615384615, "y1": 0.7276264591439688}, {"text": "A Periodic Table, a Data Sheet", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.7276264591439688, "x1": 0.46813186813186813, "y1": 0.7400778210116732}, {"text": "Total marks (15)", "x0": 0.5582417582417583, "y0": 0.7315175097276264, "x1": 0.7329670329670329, "y1": 0.7431906614785992}, {"text": "and Formulae Sheets are", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.7439688715953308, "x1": 0.4142857142857143, "y1": 0.7540856031128405}, {"text": "* Attempt Question 32", "x0": 0.5582417582417583, "y0": 0.7478599221789883, "x1": 0.8032967032967033, "y1": 0.7603112840466926}, {"text": "provided on the back pages of", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.7610894941634241, "x1": 0.46813186813186813, "y1": 0.7735408560311284}, {"text": "this paper. It may be necessary", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.777431906614786, "x1": 0.4835164835164835, "y1": 0.7898832684824902}, {"text": "* Allow about 25 minutes for this", "x0": 0.5582417582417583, "y0": 0.7813229571984436, "x1": 0.9153846153846154, "y1": 0.7906614785992218}, {"text": "to you to refer to these to", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.7945525291828793, "x1": 0.4230769230769231, "y1": 0.8070038910505837}, {"text": "part", "x0": 0.589010989010989, "y0": 0.798443579766537, "x1": 0.6296703296703297, "y1": 0.8101167315175097}, {"text": "answer some of the questions in", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.8108949416342413, "x1": 0.49230769230769234, "y1": 0.8233463035019455}, {"text": "this examination paper.", "x0": 0.15604395604395604, "y0": 0.8272373540856031, "x1": 0.4010989010989011, "y1": 0.8396887159533074}, {"text": "* Write your answers in the", "x0": 0.5582417582417583, "y0": 0.8303501945525292, "x1": 0.8571428571428571, "y1": 0.8428015564202335}, {"text": "spaces provided on the paper", "x0": 0.589010989010989, "y0": 0.846692607003891, "x1": 0.8945054945054945, "y1": 0.8591439688715953}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 2", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "1. Rockets work better in space than in the Earth\u2019s atmosphere", "x0": 0.15054945054945054, "y0": 0.10817120622568094, "x1": 0.6483516483516484, "y1": 0.11906614785992217}, {"text": "in a vacuum there is nothing to push against", "x0": 0.22637362637362637, "y0": 0.133852140077821, "x1": 0.5648351648351648, "y1": 0.14396887159533073}, {"text": "momentum of the rocket and its burnt fuel is conserved", "x0": 0.22637362637362637, "y0": 0.14863813229571984, "x1": 0.6494505494505495, "y1": 0.15642023346303502}, {"text": "the rocket has used most of its fuel and its mass is less", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.16342412451361868, "x1": 0.6472527472527473, "y1": 0.17120622568093385}, {"text": "friction drops to almost nothing.", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.177431906614786, "x1": 0.46923076923076923, "y1": 0.18754863813229572}, {"text": "2 The slingshot effect is used to increase the speed of a spacecraft as it approaches, swings", "x0": 0.15494505494505495, "y0": 0.22023346303501945, "x1": 0.8714285714285714, "y1": 0.23035019455252917}, {"text": "around and leaves a planet. Which statement is correct?", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.23346303501945526, "x1": 0.6164835164835165, "y1": 0.24357976653696498}, {"text": "A The energy of the planet stays constant.", "x0": 0.17582417582417584, "y0": 0.2614785992217899, "x1": 0.4978021978021978, "y1": 0.2715953307392996}, {"text": "B There is no energy loss or gain for the planet-spacecraft system.", "x0": 0.17802197802197803, "y0": 0.2747081712062257, "x1": 0.7164835164835165, "y1": 0.2848249027237354}, {"text": "C The momentum of the planet is constant.", "x0": 0.17802197802197803, "y0": 0.28793774319066145, "x1": 0.5384615384615384, "y1": 0.2980544747081712}, {"text": "D The potential energy of the spacecraft is constant.", "x0": 0.17912087912087912, "y0": 0.30116731517509726, "x1": 0.6065934065934065, "y1": 0.311284046692607}, {"text": "3 It is not usually possible to communicate with spacecraft as they are reentering earth\u2019s", "x0": 0.15494505494505495, "y0": 0.33929961089494165, "x1": 0.8505494505494505, "y1": 0.35019455252918286}, {"text": "atmosphere because", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.35330739299610897, "x1": 0.34395604395604396, "y1": 0.36342412451361866}, {"text": "the astronauts are too busy", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.3665369649805447, "x1": 0.4351648351648352, "y1": 0.37665369649805447}, {"text": "the spacecraft gets too hot as it renters", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.38132295719844356, "x1": 0.5351648351648352, "y1": 0.3914396887159533}, {"text": "ionised air blocks any radio signal.", "x0": 0.22637362637362637, "y0": 0.3953307392996109, "x1": 0.4857142857142857, "y1": 0.4054474708171206}, {"text": "any radio aerial would get ripped off.", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.4101167315175097, "x1": 0.5043956043956044, "y1": 0.42023346303501946}, {"text": "4 Awire carrying a current is diagram perpendicular to the page, with current running into the", "x0": 0.15384615384615385, "y0": 0.4529182879377432, "x1": 0.8923076923076924, "y1": 0.46303501945525294}, {"text": "page. Which diagram indicates the shape and direction of the magnetic field near the", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.466147859922179, "x1": 0.8417582417582418, "y1": 0.4762645914396887}, {"text": "current?", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.4785992217898833, "x1": 0.24945054945054945, "y1": 0.48638132295719844}, {"text": "A B", "x0": 0.2175824175824176, "y0": 0.5050583657587548, "x1": 0.5846153846153846, "y1": 0.51284046692607}, {"text": "c D", "x0": 0.2175824175824176, "y0": 0.6249027237354086, "x1": 0.5857142857142857, "y1": 0.6326848249027237}, {"text": "A positive charge is moving parallel to a uniform magnetic field. Which of the following is", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8155642023346303, "x1": 0.8648351648351649, "y1": 0.82568093385214}, {"text": "a", "x0": 0.15494505494505495, "y0": 0.8155642023346303, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.8233463035019455}, {"text": "true?", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8280155642023346, "x1": 0.22527472527472528, "y1": 0.8357976653696498}, {"text": "There is no force on the charge", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8443579766536965, "x1": 0.42527472527472526, "y1": 0.8544747081712062}, {"text": "The direction of a force is given by the right hand rule", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8607003891050584, "x1": 0.5989010989010989, "y1": 0.8708171206225681}, {"text": "The force makes the particle move in a circular path", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8708171206225681, "x1": 0.5846153846153846, "y1": 0.8933852140077821}, {"text": "The force makes the particle move in an helical path.", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8941634241245137, "x1": 0.5912087912087912, "y1": 0.9042801556420234}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 3", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "6", "x0": 0.15494505494505495, "y0": 0.10428015564202335, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.11206225680933853}, {"text": "In an electric motor the purpose of the split ring commutator is to;", "x0": 0.189010989010989, "y0": 0.10428015564202335, "x1": 0.6956043956043956, "y1": 0.11439688715953307}, {"text": "change the AC supply to a DC supply", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.12918287937743192, "x1": 0.5065934065934066, "y1": 0.13929961089494164}, {"text": "reverse the flow of current though the coil", "x0": 0.22637362637362637, "y0": 0.14396887159533073, "x1": 0.545054945054945, "y1": 0.15408560311284047}, {"text": "provide a complete circuit for current to reach the coil", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.15797665369649805, "x1": 0.6351648351648351, "y1": 0.1680933852140078}, {"text": "anchors the ends of the coil.", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.17276264591439688, "x1": 0.43956043956043955, "y1": 0.18054474708171206}, {"text": "W. H. Bragg and W. L. Bragg are of interest in the development of scientific ideas because", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.22957198443579765, "x1": 0.8758241758241758, "y1": 0.2396887159533074}, {"text": "they", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.24280155642023346, "x1": 0.21978021978021978, "y1": 0.2529182879377432}, {"text": "worked with Rutherford on alpha rays", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.25603112840466924, "x1": 0.512087912087912, "y1": 0.266147859922179}, {"text": "produced the technique of X-ray crystallography", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.2708171206225681, "x1": 0.5923076923076923, "y1": 0.2809338521400778}, {"text": "shared a Nobel prize in 1915", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.2856031128404669, "x1": 0.44285714285714284, "y1": 0.29571984435797666}, {"text": "showed the Australian government the importance of Science", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.29961089494163423, "x1": 0.6934065934065934, "y1": 0.309727626459144}, {"text": "The technical development that led to the production and investigation of cathode rays was;", "x0": 0.18681318681318682, "y0": 0.35642023346303503, "x1": 0.889010989010989, "y1": 0.3665369649805447}, {"text": "A the invention of good vacuum pumps", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.37042801556420235, "x1": 0.5043956043956044, "y1": 0.38054474708171204}, {"text": "B the building of high voltage batteries", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.3852140077821012, "x1": 0.5021978021978022, "y1": 0.3953307392996109}, {"text": "C the use of photography to record results", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.3992217898832685, "x1": 0.5296703296703297, "y1": 0.4093385214007782}, {"text": "the ability to blow glass into thin tubes.", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.4140077821011673, "x1": 0.521978021978022, "y1": 0.42412451361867703}, {"text": "Superconductors", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.4708171206225681, "x1": 0.3142857142857143, "y1": 0.48093385214007783}, {"text": "A can operate in the region of large magnetic fields", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.4848249027237354, "x1": 0.5989010989010989, "y1": 0.49494163424124515}, {"text": "B can carry currents without getting hot", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.49883268482490273, "x1": 0.5131868131868131, "y1": 0.5089494163424124}, {"text": "C are easy to make into superconducting magnets", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.5136186770428015, "x1": 0.5912087912087912, "y1": 0.5237354085603113}, {"text": "D need to be cooled to the temperature of liquid helium.", "x0": 0.17802197802197803, "y0": 0.5276264591439689, "x1": 0.6362637362637362, "y1": 0.5377431906614786}, {"text": "10", "x0": 0.14615384615384616, "y0": 0.5852140077821012, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.5929961089494163}, {"text": "Doping of a piece of pure silicon with a small amount of phosphorus produces a", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.5852140077821012, "x1": 0.7923076923076923, "y1": 0.5953307392996109}, {"text": "semiconductor in which;", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.5976653696498054, "x1": 0.37142857142857144, "y1": 0.6070038910505836}, {"text": "electrons are positive charge carriers", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.6116731517509728, "x1": 0.5032967032967033, "y1": 0.6217898832684825}, {"text": "com>", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.6116731517509728, "x1": 0.1879120879120879, "y1": 0.6607003891050583}, {"text": "holes are positive charge carriers", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.6264591439688716, "x1": 0.4747252747252747, "y1": 0.6365758754863813}, {"text": "electrons are negative charge carriers", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.6396887159533073, "x1": 0.5131868131868131, "y1": 0.6498054474708171}, {"text": "holes are negative charge carriers", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.6529182879377432, "x1": 0.4835164835164835, "y1": 0.6630350194552529}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 4", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "1\"", "x0": 0.14615384615384616, "y0": 0.09260700389105059, "x1": 0.15934065934065933, "y1": 0.10038910505836576}, {"text": "An electromagnetic radiation source is held above a clean strip of zinc connected to an", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.09260700389105059, "x1": 0.8461538461538461, "y1": 0.10272373540856031}, {"text": "electrically charged electroscope.", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.10505836575875487, "x1": 0.43846153846153846, "y1": 0.11517509727626458}, {"text": "radiation", "x0": 0.367032967032967, "y0": 0.17509727626459143, "x1": 0.4307692307692308, "y1": 0.1828793774319066}, {"text": "electroscope", "x0": 0.378021978021978, "y0": 0.23346303501945526, "x1": 0.4714285714285714, "y1": 0.24357976653696498}, {"text": "The electroscope is most likely to lose its charge if", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.36342412451361866, "x1": 0.510989010989011, "y1": 0.3735408560311284}, {"text": "the light source produces red light and the electroscope is positively charged", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.377431906614786, "x1": 0.7604395604395604, "y1": 0.38754863813229573}, {"text": "the light source produced red light and the electroscope is negatively charged", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.3922178988326848, "x1": 0.7692307692307693, "y1": 0.40233463035019457}, {"text": "the light source produces ultraviolet light and the electroscope is positively charged", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.40622568093385214, "x1": 0.8120879120879121, "y1": 0.4163424124513619}, {"text": "the light source produces ultraviolet light and the electroscope is positively charged", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.421011673151751, "x1": 0.8120879120879121, "y1": 0.4311284046692607}, {"text": "12", "x0": 0.14615384615384616, "y0": 0.4840466926070039, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.49182879377431904}, {"text": "Radio waves are produced by", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.4840466926070039, "x1": 0.4098901098901099, "y1": 0.49416342412451364}, {"text": "electrons rapidly decelerating", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.4972762645914397, "x1": 0.44835164835164837, "y1": 0.5073929961089494}, {"text": "energy changes inside the nuclei of atoms", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.5120622568093385, "x1": 0.545054945054945, "y1": 0.5221789883268483}, {"text": "charges jumping across energy levels in an atom", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.5268482490272374, "x1": 0.5967032967032967, "y1": 0.5369649805447471}, {"text": "oscillating electric charges.", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.5408560311284046, "x1": 0.42857142857142855, "y1": 0.5509727626459144}, {"text": "13", "x0": 0.14615384615384616, "y0": 0.5976653696498054, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.6054474708171206}, {"text": "The Zeeman effect is concerned with the splitting of spectral lines due to", "x0": 0.18681318681318682, "y0": 0.5976653696498054, "x1": 0.7417582417582418, "y1": 0.6077821011673151}, {"text": "gravitational fields", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.6116731517509728, "x1": 0.3648351648351648, "y1": 0.6217898832684825}, {"text": "electric fields", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.6264591439688716, "x1": 0.3241758241758242, "y1": 0.6342412451361867}, {"text": "magnetic fields", "x0": 0.22637362637362637, "y0": 0.6404669260700389, "x1": 0.3384615384615385, "y1": 0.6505836575875487}, {"text": "relative motion.", "x0": 0.22637362637362637, "y0": 0.6552529182879377, "x1": 0.34285714285714286, "y1": 0.6630350194552529}, {"text": "What is the structure of the atom '\u00b0,C?", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.7066147859922179, "x1": 0.4846153846153846, "y1": 0.7190661478599222}, {"text": "14", "x0": 0.14615384615384616, "y0": 0.7089494163424125, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.7167315175097276}, {"text": "6 protons, 7 neutrons, 6 electrons", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.7361867704280156, "x1": 0.48131868131868133, "y1": 0.7463035019455253}, {"text": "6 protons, 6 neutrons, 7 electrons", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.7501945525291829, "x1": 0.48131868131868133, "y1": 0.7603112840466926}, {"text": "6 protons, 13 neutrons, 6 electrons", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.7649805447470817, "x1": 0.4912087912087912, "y1": 0.7750972762645915}, {"text": "13 protons, 6 neutrons, 13 electrons", "x0": 0.22417582417582418, "y0": 0.7797665369649806, "x1": 0.5010989010989011, "y1": 0.7898832684824902}, {"text": "For the nuclear reaction \u2018on + *4,.Mg x Y + \u201cSe", "x0": 0.1879120879120879, "y0": 0.8342412451361868, "x1": 0.6428571428571429, "y1": 0.846692607003891}, {"text": "15", "x0": 0.14615384615384616, "y0": 0.8365758754863813, "x1": 0.16263736263736264, "y1": 0.8443579766536965}, {"text": "X is magnesium, Y is aluminium", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.863035019455253, "x1": 0.46483516483516485, "y1": 0.8731517509727627}, {"text": "com>", "x0": 0.17692307692307693, "y0": 0.863035019455253, "x1": 0.1879120879120879, "y1": 0.9120622568093385}, {"text": "X is magnesium, Yis sodium", "x0": 0.2230769230769231, "y0": 0.8778210116731517, "x1": 0.43956043956043955, "y1": 0.8879377431906614}, {"text": "X is aluminium, Y is magnesium", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.8910505836575876, "x1": 0.46703296703296704, "y1": 0.9011673151750973}, {"text": "X is aluminium, Yis silicon", "x0": 0.22527472527472528, "y0": 0.9042801556420234, "x1": 0.42527472527472526, "y1": 0.9136186770428015}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 5", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Part B", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.0933852140077821, "x1": 0.1956043956043956, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "Total marks (70)", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.12684824902723735, "x1": 0.3021978021978022, "y1": 0.13852140077821012}, {"text": "Attempt Questions 15\u201431", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.15953307392996108, "x1": 0.3956043956043956, "y1": 0.17198443579766537}, {"text": "Allow about 120 minutes for this part", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.18521400778210118, "x1": 0.5175824175824176, "y1": 0.21011673151750973}, {"text": "Write your answers in the appropriate space on the paper. Marks", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.22568093385214008, "x1": 0.8945054945054945, "y1": 0.23813229571984434}, {"text": "allocated to each question are shown in parentheses following the", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.24202334630350195, "x1": 0.8945054945054945, "y1": 0.2544747081712062}, {"text": "question. Show all relevant working in questions involving calculations", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.25758754863813227, "x1": 0.8945054945054945, "y1": 0.2708171206225681}, {"text": "will hi for not showing working).", "x0": 0.24725274725274726, "y0": 0.267704280155642, "x1": 0.6945054945054945, "y1": 0.2926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 16 (4 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.34007782101167316, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.35252918287937746}, {"text": "a Calculate the acceleration due to gravity on the surface of Mars.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.3688715953307393, "x1": 0.6505494505494506, "y1": 0.378988326848249}, {"text": "Mass of Mars = 6.42 X 107 kg", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.39455252918287936, "x1": 0.4087912087912088, "y1": 0.40778210116731517}, {"text": "Radius of Mars = 3.39 x 10\u00b0m", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.40622568093385214, "x1": 0.4043956043956044, "y1": 0.4272373540856031}, {"text": "b For the moons of Jupiter, Ganymede and lo:", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.5961089494163424, "x1": 0.5021978021978022, "y1": 0.6062256809338521}, {"text": "Radius of orbit of Ganymede = 1.07 x 10\u00b0 km", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.622568093385214, "x1": 0.5197802197802198, "y1": 0.6357976653696498}, {"text": "Period for one revolution of Ganymede = 7.15 days", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.6544747081712062, "x1": 0.554945054945055, "y1": 0.6645914396887159}, {"text": "Radius of revolution of lo = 4.22 x 10\u00b0 km", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.6809338521400778, "x1": 0.5, "y1": 0.6910505836575875}, {"text": "Calculate the period of revolution of lo about the planet Mars (in days).", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.7120622568093385, "x1": 0.7076923076923077, "y1": 0.7221789883268482}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 6", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 17 (3 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09260700389105059, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.10505836575875487}, {"text": "For the Moon:", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.12918287937743192, "x1": 0.23296703296703297, "y1": 0.1369649805447471}, {"text": "Radius of orbit about the Earth = 3.84 x 10\u00b0m", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.14785992217898833, "x1": 0.4945054945054945, "y1": 0.15797665369649805}, {"text": "Period for one revolution = 2.36 X 10\u00b0s", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.1688715953307393, "x1": 0.4461538461538462, "y1": 0.17898832684824903}, {"text": "Calculate the value of the gravitational acceleration due to the Earth at the surface of the Moon.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.19221789883268484, "x1": 0.8593406593406593, "y1": 0.20233463035019456}, {"text": "Question 18 (2 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.3898832684824903, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.40233463035019457}, {"text": "It is sometimes stated that the objects in a satellite orbiting the Earth are in \u201cfree fall\u2019.", "x0": 0.12967032967032968, "y0": 0.42568093385214006, "x1": 0.7857142857142857, "y1": 0.4365758754863813}, {"text": "Explain this term clearly.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.4474708171206226, "x1": 0.3153846153846154, "y1": 0.4575875486381323}, {"text": "Question 19 (3 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.6723735408560312, "x1": 0.3626373626373626, "y1": 0.6848249027237354}, {"text": "A passenger of mass 60.0 kg is standing in a lift which is accelerating up at 1.00 m s-*.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.7050583657587549, "x1": 0.7923076923076923, "y1": 0.7151750972762646}, {"text": "a Calculate the weight force of the passenger.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.7322957198443579, "x1": 0.4978021978021978, "y1": 0.7424124513618677}, {"text": "b the magnitude of the force exerted by the floor of the lift on the passenger.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.8319066147859923, "x1": 0.8131868131868132, "y1": 0.842023346303502}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 7", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 20 (5 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09260700389105059, "x1": 0.33186813186813185, "y1": 0.10272373540856031}, {"text": "A basketballer standing 8.00 m (horizontally) from the basket launches a ball from 1.50 m below", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.12607003891050583, "x1": 0.8670329670329671, "y1": 0.13618677042801555}, {"text": "the level of the basket and watches it begin to fall cleanly through the ring 1.20 s later.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.14007782101167315, "x1": 0.7934065934065934, "y1": 0.16264591439688716}, {"text": "a Calculate the horizontal velocity with which the ball was launched.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.18443579766536966, "x1": 0.667032967032967, "y1": 0.19455252918287938}, {"text": "b Calculate the vertical velocity with which the ball was launched.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.31906614785992216, "x1": 0.6472527472527473, "y1": 0.3291828793774319}, {"text": "\u00a9 Calculate the angle at which the ball was projected.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.4894941634241245, "x1": 0.5560439560439561, "y1": 0.49961089494163424}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 8", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 21 (5 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.0933852140077821, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "The time between flashes of the strobe was 0.040 s and the scale was that 10.0 mm on the photo", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.5307392996108949, "x1": 0.8747252747252747, "y1": 0.5408560311284046}, {"text": "represented 0.106 m in reality.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.5455252918287937, "x1": 0.35934065934065934, "y1": 0.5556420233463035}, {"text": "a Determine the horizontal velocity of the ball", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.5727626459143969, "x1": 0.4967032967032967, "y1": 0.5828793774319067}, {"text": "b Determine the vertical acceleration due to gravity.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.7431906614785992, "x1": 0.5417582417582417, "y1": 0.7533073929961089}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 9", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 22 (2 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09105058365758754, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.10428015564202335}, {"text": "The diagram represents 2 current carrying wires, with currents into the page.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.12762645914396886, "x1": 0.7175824175824176, "y1": 0.1377431906614786}, {"text": "Draw the shape of the resulting magnetic field that forms due to the overlap of the field of each", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.1424124513618677, "x1": 0.8571428571428571, "y1": 0.15252918287937745}, {"text": "wire.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.15797665369649805, "x1": 0.16373626373626374, "y1": 0.16342412451361868}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 10", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 23 (5 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09105058365758754, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "The diagram represents two circuits with 4.0 A of current flowing through each of them.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.12762645914396886, "x1": 0.7978021978021979, "y1": 0.1377431906614786}, {"text": "Section BC is 0.01 m vertically above section GH. Lengths BC and GH are each 0.03 m.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.14863813229571984, "x1": 0.7978021978021979, "y1": 0.15875486381322956}, {"text": "FGHI is a beam pivoted at F and I.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.16964980544747083, "x1": 0.3901098901098901, "y1": 0.17976653696498054}, {"text": "m, is placed on the other end at an equal distance from the pivot as GH.", "x0": 0.1945054945054945, "y0": 0.19143968871595332, "x1": 0.7450549450549451, "y1": 0.20155642023346304}, {"text": "A B", "x0": 0.34835164835164834, "y0": 0.2272373540856031, "x1": 0.4879120879120879, "y1": 0.23501945525291829}, {"text": "+", "x0": 0.31978021978021975, "y0": 0.24280155642023346, "x1": 0.3362637362637363, "y1": 0.2529182879377432}, {"text": "D", "x0": 0.3076923076923077, "y0": 0.3066147859922179, "x1": 0.321978021978022, "y1": 0.3159533073929961}, {"text": "H T", "x0": 0.44175824175824174, "y0": 0.34007782101167316, "x1": 0.6, "y1": 0.354863813229572}, {"text": "J", "x0": 0.5406593406593406, "y0": 0.37976653696498053, "x1": 0.5472527472527473, "y1": 0.38754863813229573}, {"text": "+", "x0": 0.5263736263736264, "y0": 0.3953307392996109, "x1": 0.5373626373626373, "y1": 0.40077821011673154}, {"text": "a Calculate the magnetic force acting between BC and GH.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.40700389105058365, "x1": 0.5934065934065934, "y1": 0.4171206225680934}, {"text": "b Calculate the size of the mass m which should be placed to exactly balance this magnetic force.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.5774319066147859, "x1": 0.8912087912087913, "y1": 0.5875486381322957}, {"text": "\u00a9 why is it possible to ignore any forces between AD and GH.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.7470817120622568, "x1": 0.6736263736263737, "y1": 0.7571984435797665}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 11", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8857142857142857, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 24 (5 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09260700389105059, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.10505836575875487}, {"text": "A coil of 120 turns is placed between the poles of a magnet, producing a magnetic field in the plane", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.12762645914396886, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.1377431906614786}, {"text": "of the page, as in the diagram. The magnetic field penetrating the coil is 3.00 x 10\u201d T. The coil is,", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.1463035019455253, "x1": 0.8758241758241758, "y1": 0.15875486381322956}, {"text": "square, of side 4.00 x 10% m.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.16731517509727625, "x1": 0.35384615384615387, "y1": 0.17976653696498054}, {"text": ">", "x0": 0.6626373626373626, "y0": 0.20622568093385213, "x1": 0.6846153846153846, "y1": 0.21945525291828794}, {"text": "coil", "x0": 0.5923076923076923, "y0": 0.24046692607003892, "x1": 0.6197802197802198, "y1": 0.25136186770428015}, {"text": "current", "x0": 0.2802197802197802, "y0": 0.354863813229572, "x1": 0.3362637362637363, "y1": 0.3642023346303502}, {"text": "magnetic field", "x0": 0.5868131868131868, "y0": 0.37976653696498053, "x1": 0.6967032967032967, "y1": 0.39377431906614785}, {"text": "axis of rotation", "x0": 0.35494505494505496, "y0": 0.40077821011673154, "x1": 0.47692307692307695, "y1": 0.41167315175097274}, {"text": "(out of page)", "x0": 0.3505494505494505, "y0": 0.4171206225680934, "x1": 0.44835164835164837, "y1": 0.4272373540856031}, {"text": "a Calculate the force on side PQ when a current of 6.00 A flows through it.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.4490272373540856, "x1": 0.7164835164835165, "y1": 0.4708171206225681}, {"text": "b the torque causing the coil to turn when in the position shown.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.6256809338521401, "x1": 0.7153846153846154, "y1": 0.6357976653696498}, {"text": "\u00a9 Describe a modification that might be used to make the torque of the coil constant.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.7961089494163424, "x1": 0.798901098901099, "y1": 0.8062256809338522}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 12", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 25 (2 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09260700389105059, "x1": 0.34175824175824177, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "Identify two advantages of AC induction motors compared to DC motors.", "x0": 0.12967032967032968, "y0": 0.12762645914396886, "x1": 0.6824175824175824, "y1": 0.1377431906614786}, {"text": "Question 26 (7 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.3953307392996109, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.40778210116731517}, {"text": "a __ Describe an experiment of Michael Faraday which demonstrated that electricity could be", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.41945525291828795, "x1": 0.8351648351648352, "y1": 0.44202334630350193}, {"text": "generated using magnetism.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.4412451361867704, "x1": 0.3802197802197802, "y1": 0.45136186770428016}, {"text": "wire 2.0 m long is moved at 4.0 at right angles to a magnetic field of 3.0 x 10\u00b0", "x0": 0.24285714285714285, "y0": 0.6910505836575875, "x1": 0.8945054945054945, "y1": 0.7035019455252919}, {"text": "T.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.708171206225681, "x1": 0.17692307692307693, "y1": 0.7159533073929961}, {"text": "Calculate the magnitude of the emf generated across the ends of the wire.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.7221789883268482, "x1": 0.7307692307692307, "y1": 0.7322957198443579}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 13", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "\u00a9 A flat sheet of copper is held vertically and then dropped so that it enters a strong horizontal", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.0933852140077821, "x1": 0.8703296703296703, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "magnetic field.", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.10739299610894941, "x1": 0.27472527472527475, "y1": 0.11750972762645914}, {"text": "Complete the graph showing the downward acceleration of the copper sheet.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.13618677042801555, "x1": 0.7483516483516484, "y1": 0.1463035019455253}, {"text": "10", "x0": 0.2835164835164835, "y0": 0.19221789883268484, "x1": 0.3010989010989011, "y1": 0.2}, {"text": "downward", "x0": 0.2043956043956044, "y0": 0.26381322957198444, "x1": 0.289010989010989, "y1": 0.2723735408560311}, {"text": "acceleration", "x0": 0.1967032967032967, "y0": 0.2785992217898833, "x1": 0.2967032967032967, "y1": 0.28715953307392994}, {"text": "before entering | falling within after leaving |", "x0": 0.35494505494505496, "y0": 0.39455252918287936, "x1": 0.8813186813186813, "y1": 0.4093385214007782}, {"text": "the field the field the field", "x0": 0.3824175824175824, "y0": 0.41245136186770426, "x1": 0.8241758241758241, "y1": 0.42023346303501946}, {"text": "Position of sheet", "x0": 0.5340659340659341, "y0": 0.43813229571984436, "x1": 0.6747252747252748, "y1": 0.44591439688715956}, {"text": "Explain the shape of your graph.", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.4778210116731518, "x1": 0.41098901098901097, "y1": 0.48793774319066147}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 14", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 27 (4 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.0980544747081712, "x1": 0.34175824175824177, "y1": 0.10894941634241245}, {"text": "A transformer has 400 turns on the primary and 100 turns on the secondary.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.133852140077821, "x1": 0.7153846153846154, "y1": 0.14396887159533073}, {"text": "If 240 V AC is applied to the primary and a current of 0.40 A flows through the primary", "x0": 0.12967032967032968, "y0": 0.1556420233463035, "x1": 0.7934065934065934, "y1": 0.16575875486381322}, {"text": "a Calculate the voltage available across the secondary coil.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.19221789883268484, "x1": 0.5967032967032967, "y1": 0.20233463035019456}, {"text": "b Calculate the maximum current available from the secondary coil.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.36264591439688715, "x1": 0.6648351648351648, "y1": 0.3727626459143969}, {"text": "\u00a9 When electricity is carried across large distances it is conveyed by transmission lines operating", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.5330739299610895, "x1": 0.8857142857142857, "y1": 0.5431906614785992}, {"text": "at 110 000 volts or higher.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.5470817120622569, "x1": 0.367032967032967, "y1": 0.5571984435797666}, {"text": "Identify an advantage that is gained by operating at such high voltages.", "x0": 0.16593406593406593, "y0": 0.5618677042801556, "x1": 0.7131868131868132, "y1": 0.5719844357976653}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 15", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 28 (6 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.10428015564202335, "x1": 0.34175824175824177, "y1": 0.11517509727626458}, {"text": "With respect to the photoelectric effect:", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.13929961089494164, "x1": 0.42967032967032964, "y1": 0.14941634241245136}, {"text": "a explain what was predicted by classical theory for light falling on a metal surface.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.17587548638132297, "x1": 0.7835164835164835, "y1": 0.1859922178988327}, {"text": "b describe what is actually observed in photoelectric experiments.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.3961089494163424, "x1": 0.6505494505494506, "y1": 0.40622568093385214}, {"text": "\u00a9 explain how quantum theory can be used to reconcile the experimental observations.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.6163424124513619, "x1": 0.8120879120879121, "y1": 0.6264591439688716}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.378021978021978, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Page 16", "x0": 0.8285714285714286, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 29 (8 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.0980544747081712, "x1": 0.34175824175824177, "y1": 0.10894941634241245}, {"text": "Use band theory to explain the difference in electrical conductivity between metallic conductors", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.12918287937743192, "x1": 0.889010989010989, "y1": 0.13929961089494164}, {"text": "a", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.13151750972762646, "x1": 0.13626373626373625, "y1": 0.1369649805447471}, {"text": "and insulators.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.1431906614785992, "x1": 0.27472527472527475, "y1": 0.1509727626459144}, {"text": "Explain the effect of \u2018doping\u2019 have on the band structure of silicon atoms.", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.4171206225680934, "x1": 0.7208791208791209, "y1": 0.4280155642023346}, {"text": "b", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.4178988326848249, "x1": 0.13626373626373625, "y1": 0.42568093385214006}, {"text": "Add 2 sets of positive + and negative - charges to indicate", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.6926070038910506, "x1": 0.6087912087912087, "y1": 0.7027237354085603}, {"text": "* the movement of electric charge carriers", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.7073929961089495, "x1": 0.4857142857142857, "y1": 0.7175097276264591}, {"text": "* the battery terminals", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.7214007782101167, "x1": 0.3373626373626374, "y1": 0.7315175097276264}, {"text": "when a p-n junction is connected to a battery, as in the diagram.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.7361867704280156, "x1": 0.6582417582417582, "y1": 0.7463035019455253}, {"text": "P type", "x0": 0.4, "y0": 0.8357976653696498, "x1": 0.4472527472527473, "y1": 0.845136186770428}, {"text": "n type", "x0": 0.5758241758241758, "y0": 0.8357976653696498, "x1": 0.621978021978022, "y1": 0.845136186770428}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 17", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "d Outline 2 different applications of solid state devices (e.g. transistors, integrated circuits) which", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.0933852140077821, "x1": 0.8912087912087913, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "have changed or are changing our society.", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.10739299610894941, "x1": 0.4868131868131868, "y1": 0.11750972762645914}, {"text": "Question 30 (2 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.3906614785992218, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.4031128404669261}, {"text": "a __ the purpose of the Miche1son-Morley experiment.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.41478599221789886, "x1": 0.6120879120879121, "y1": 0.43735408560311284}, {"text": "b Discuss how Einstein's relativity concept explains the result of the Michelson-Morley", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.5914396887159533, "x1": 0.8010989010989011, "y1": 0.6023346303501945}, {"text": "experiment.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.6077821011673151, "x1": 0.25384615384615383, "y1": 0.6171206225680934}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 18", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Question 31 (6 marks)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09105058365758754, "x1": 0.41208791208791207, "y1": 0.10350194552529182}, {"text": "Sam is sitting on a railway station 200.00 m long.", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.12762645914396886, "x1": 0.5065934065934066, "y1": 0.1377431906614786}, {"text": "Tom is in a train 200.00 m long, moving through the station.", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.14863813229571984, "x1": 0.5912087912087912, "y1": 0.15875486381322956}, {"text": "If the train could travel at 0.01\u00a2 (i.e. at 1% of the speed of light relative to the station), calculate", "x0": 0.12967032967032968, "y0": 0.16964980544747083, "x1": 0.8736263736263736, "y1": 0.17976653696498054}, {"text": "a how long Tom would perceive the station to be", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.20700389105058364, "x1": 0.5197802197802198, "y1": 0.2171206225680934}, {"text": "b how long Sam would perceive the station to be", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.377431906614786, "x1": 0.5208791208791209, "y1": 0.38754863813229573}, {"text": "\u00a9 long Tom would observe the front of the train taking to pass through the station", "x0": 0.12747252747252746, "y0": 0.5470817120622569, "x1": 0.810989010989011, "y1": 0.5571984435797666}, {"text": "long Sam would see the front of the train taking to pass through the station", "x0": 0.2010989010989011, "y0": 0.7175097276264591, "x1": 0.778021978021978, "y1": 0.7276264591439688}], [{"text": "Barker College Trial Physics 2001 Page 19", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.05914396887159533, "x1": 0.8879120879120879, "y1": 0.06926070038910506}, {"text": "Part C From Quanta to Quarks Total marks (15)", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.09649805447470818, "x1": 0.6263736263736264, "y1": 0.10894941634241245}, {"text": "Attempt Question 32", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.12996108949416343, "x1": 0.34285714285714286, "y1": 0.1431906614785992}, {"text": "(a) Identify THREE limitations of the Bohr model of the atom published in 1913. 3", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.20933852140077822, "x1": 0.8703296703296703, "y1": 0.21945525291828794}, {"text": "(b) Determine the wavelength of the radiation produced when an electron in the hydrogen 3", "x0": 0.12857142857142856, "y0": 0.4785992217898833, "x1": 0.8703296703296703, "y1": 0.488715953307393}, {"text": "atom moves from the fifth energy level to the third energy level", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.4933852140077821, "x1": 0.6494505494505495, "y1": 0.5035019455252918}, {"text": "Discuss the development of theories of the structure of the atom with reference to the 9", "x0": 0.16483516483516483, "y0": 0.6926070038910506, "x1": 0.8703296703296703, "y1": 0.7027237354085603}, {"text": "work of all three of Thomson, Rutherford and Bohr.", "x0": 0.16373626373626374, "y0": 0.7066147859922179, "x1": 0.5527472527472528, "y1": 0.7159533073929961}, {"text": "In each case, indicate one piece of experimental evidence which supported the idea.", "x0": 0.16593406593406593, "y0": 0.7361867704280156, "x1": 0.8054945054945055, "y1": 0.7463035019455253}, {"text": "END OF PAPER", "x0": 0.4197802197802198, "y0": 0.8793774319066148, "x1": 0.5703296703296703, "y1": 0.8894941634241245}]]